WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.460
A secure Prisma review starts by locating every place Prisma Client is used.

2
00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:07.560
Look beyond route handlers.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:22.780
Prisma calls may live in GraphQL resolvers, service layers, repositories, background workers, queue consumers, scripts, seed jobs, migrations, admin tools, tests, and serverless functions.

4
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:36.400
A Prisma call that looks clean by itself may still be risky if it receives values from a public request, a webhook, a support form, a queue message, a configuration value, or a previously stored user-controlled field.

5
00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:54.840
Map the input sources that can influence each query or mutation: route parameters, query strings, request bodies, headers, cookies, uploaded files, queue messages, webhook payloads, existing database records, configuration, and admin forms.

6
00:00:54.940 --> 00:01:01.020
Treat externally supplied values as untrusted even after they pass through several application layers.

7
00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:17.440
Then identify sensitive models and fields, such as users, sessions, roles, permissions, tenant IDs, billing data, audit records, reset tokens, API keys, private documents, and security settings.

8
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.200
The important trust-boundary question is not simply whether a Prisma call compiles.

9
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:31.580
Ask who supplied the value, which model and fields it can affect, what authorization has already happened, what database identity is being used, and what fields will be returned.

10
00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:35.680
Prisma security should be reviewed at the workflow level.

11
00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:46.500
The repository call, the service logic, the route authorization, the validation layer, the database account, and the response mapping together decide whether a feature is safe.
